= Sir Keith Joseph

In charge of policy and research, co-founder of the Centre for Policy Studies, and the intellectual engine of the new Conservatism. He might have been leader himself, had his speeches been better judged.

- @lecture_tour
- @stepping_stones
- @root: Return

@lecture_tour
title: University lecture tour
subtitle: Raises public support for free-market economics; the dries grow stronger.
choose-if: advisor_action_timer = 0
unavailable-subtitle: [+ advisor_action_timer +] months before the next Shadow Cabinet action.
on-arrival: advisor_action_timer = 6; last_advisor_action = 1; economic_liberalism += 5; dry_strength += 4; wet_dissent += 3

Sir Keith lectures on campuses across the country, sometimes over the shouting of left-wing students. The arguments get reported, and some of them stick.

@stepping_stones
title: "Stepping Stones"
view-if: policy_union_reform = 0
subtitle: Adopts trade union reform at once, with a strategy to sell it.
choose-if: advisor_action_timer = 0
unavailable-subtitle: [+ advisor_action_timer +] months before the next Shadow Cabinet action.
on-arrival: advisor_action_timer = 6; last_advisor_action = 1; policy_union_reform = 1; dry_strength += 3; wet_dissent += 6; union_backlash += 2

Sir Keith champions the confidential "Stepping Stones" report: the unions are the obstacle to recovery, and a Conservative government has to say so openly. Jim Prior is appalled.
